---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/sections/PageHeader.astro';
import RelatedContent from '../../components/sections/RelatedContent.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/sections/CTABand.astro';
import { buildBreadcrumbSchema, buildArticleSchema } from '../../lib/schema';

const title = 'Custom Packaging RFQ Template: 12 Required Fields';
const description = 'Use this 12-field packaging RFQ template to brief suppliers on boxes or pouches with dimensions, product needs, quantities, artwork, testing and delivery details.';
const url = '/blog/custom-packaging-rfq-template-12-specs-buyers-include';
const datePublished = '2026-07-09';
const dateModified = '2026-10-31';
const image = '/images/blog/custom-packaging-rfq-template-generated.png';

const breadcrumb = buildBreadcrumbSchema([
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Blog', url: '/blog' },
  { name: 'Custom Packaging RFQ Template', url }
]);
const article = {
  ...buildArticleSchema({ title, description, url, datePublished, author: 'Apex Packaging Solutions' }),
  dateModified
};
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} image={image} schemaBlocks={[breadcrumb, article]}>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Knowledge base · Procurement"
    heading="Custom packaging RFQ template: 12 required fields."
    lead="Use one structured brief to describe the product, package, performance, artwork, quantities, validation and delivery—without forcing suppliers to price from assumptions."
    imageSrc={image}
    imageAlt="Packaging RFQ worksheet with dieline, product measurements, material samples and printed packages."
  />

  <section class="py-12 md:py-20">
    <div class="container max-w-default prose-apex">
      <p><strong>A useful packaging RFQ makes supplier responses comparable.</strong> It distinguishes fixed requirements from open design questions, gives every SKU a clear quantity and dimension set, and states how the finished package will be approved. The aim is not to prescribe a board grade, laminate or test level from one variable; it is to provide enough context for a supplier to propose and explain a complete specification.</p>
      <p>Use the 12 fields below for boxes, mailers, pouches and related packaging. For a purchasing enquiry, send the completed brief through <a href="/contact">Apex’s quotation form</a> and link it to the relevant commercial page, such as <a href="/services/corrugated-boxes">corrugated boxes</a>, <a href="/services/mailer-boxes">mailer boxes</a> or <a href="/industries/custom-mylar-bags">custom bags and pouches</a>.</p>

      <figure class="my-10 overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-neutral-200 bg-white shadow-card">
        <img src={image} alt="Packaging RFQ worksheet with dieline, measuring tools, material samples and sample packages." width="1667" height="944" loading="eager" decoding="async" class="w-full" />
        <figcaption class="px-5 py-4 text-sm text-neutral-600">Keep requirements, assumptions and supplier recommendations in separate columns so each quotation can be reviewed on the same basis.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>The 12-field packaging RFQ</h2>
      <p>The examples are hypothetical. Replace them with your own product and route data.</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[960px]">
          <thead><tr><th>Required field</th><th>What to provide</th><th>Hypothetical box example</th><th>Hypothetical pouch example</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>1. Product and packed contents</strong></td><td>Product name, components, unit weight, count per pack, photos, sensitivities and production tolerances.</td><td>Two glass jars plus a leaflet; total packed contents 1.8 kg; glass must not contact glass.</td><td>Roasted whole-bean coffee; 340 g fill; aroma and oxygen exposure are product concerns.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>2. Finished package dimensions and fit</strong></td><td>Required internal or external dimensions, dimension order, tolerances and whether the size is fixed or open to recommendation.</td><td>Internal fit must include jars, divider and top pad; supplier to recommend external size.</td><td>Target fill quantity and shelf footprint supplied; supplier to recommend finished width, height and gusset.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>3. Package format and use</strong></td><td>Package type, primary or secondary role, shelf or shipping use, opening and user experience.</td><td>Printed mailer used as the parcel shipper and customer-facing pack.</td><td>Stand-up pouch displayed at retail and opened repeatedly by the consumer.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>4. Performance requirements</strong></td><td>Protection, barrier, seal, stacking, appearance or other outcomes; state what is mandatory and what is a preference.</td><td>Prevent jar contact and maintain closure through the defined parcel test sequence.</td><td>Supplier to propose oxygen, moisture and seal performance for the stated shelf-life validation plan.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>5. Quantities and SKU split</strong></td><td>Initial quantity by size/artwork, forecast reorder quantity and cadence; label estimates as estimates.</td><td>Three artworks with the quantity for each and a separate annual forecast.</td><td>Two fill sizes, each with its own first-order quantity and estimated quarterly reorder.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>6. Filling and packing process</strong></td><td>Manual or automated steps, equipment, speeds, temperatures, sealing or closure method and packout location.</td><td>Manual erection and loading; pressure-sensitive tape applied by tabletop dispenser.</td><td>Vertical filling line; buyer supplies product temperature, jaw type, dwell and speed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>7. Artwork and print</strong></td><td>Artwork status, colours, print coverage, inside/outside panels, variable areas, barcode and proof expectations.</td><td>CMYK artwork in progress; outside print plus one-colour inside message; shipping-label zone reserved.</td><td>Front and back artwork supplied; lot-code zone and zipper clearance shown on the dieline.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>8. Features and components</strong></td><td>Inserts, dividers, closures, windows, hang holes, zippers, valves, spouts or other requested elements.</td><td>Divider required; exact style open to supplier recommendation.</td><td>Resealable closure requested; supplier to state placement tolerance and proposed construction.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>9. Distribution and storage</strong></td><td>Parcel, pallet, retail or export route; stacking; storage duration; relevant temperature, humidity and handling risks.</td><td>Individual parcel delivery across Canada, then indoor consumer storage.</td><td>Case-packed, palletized delivery to Canadian retail distribution centres; storage range supplied by buyer.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>10. Testing, acceptance and documentation</strong></td><td>Required standards, test conditions, pass/fail criteria, regulatory documents and substantiation for claims.</td><td>Buyer and laboratory to confirm parcel test plan; no broken jars, product contact or closure opening after test.</td><td>Request OTR, WVTR and seal data with methods and conditions, plus documentation for the intended food-contact use.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>11. Delivery and schedule</strong></td><td>Destination, delivery configuration, required in-hand date and dates for files, samples and approvals.</td><td>Deliver to Toronto; pallet constraints supplied; launch date separated from required arrival date.</td><td>Deliver to Vancouver co-packer; approval and filling-trial dates shown before production need date.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>12. Quote and approval details</strong></td><td>Buyer contact, revision number, currency, requested price breaks, tooling/freight breakdown, validity and who approves each stage.</td><td>Quote two stated quantities; show tooling, samples and freight separately; procurement approves commercial terms.</td><td>Quote each size separately; identify cylinder or plate assumptions and approval responsibilities.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Separate requirements, preferences and supplier proposals</h2>
      <p>Use three labels in the RFQ:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Required:</strong> a measurable condition the solution must meet.</li>
        <li><strong>Preferred:</strong> a desired outcome that can be traded off if the supplier explains why.</li>
        <li><strong>Supplier to propose:</strong> an area where the buyer wants a documented recommendation.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>For example, “packed shipment weighs 18 kg” is a fact. “No product damage after the agreed parcel test sequence” can be an acceptance criterion. “Use 44 ECT board” is a material instruction only if the buyer has validated and intends to control that full specification; otherwise, it may prevent a supplier from proposing a better-supported alternative.</p>

      <h2>Use one line per SKU</h2>
      <p>Do not hide several sizes or artworks inside a paragraph. Create one row per package SKU with dimensions, quantity, print version and special features. Show shared requirements—such as destination or approval contact—once, then identify exceptions. This makes price breaks and tooling assumptions easier to compare.</p>

      <h2>Attach evidence that removes guesswork</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Product drawings, photos and production-representative samples.</li>
        <li>Current packaging specification and dieline, if this is a repeat or redesign.</li>
        <li>Editable artwork, brand colour references and barcode files.</li>
        <li>Damage photos, complaint categories or packout observations when solving a problem.</li>
        <li>Existing test reports, regulatory requirements and written acceptance criteria.</li>
        <li>Delivery constraints, pallet requirements and co-packer or filling-line details.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Compare quotations on the same basis</h2>
      <p>Check whether every supplier responded to the same revision and quantities. Compare the complete proposed construction, dimensions and tolerances; print and feature assumptions; tooling and sample charges; freight basis; lead-time assumptions; test and documentation scope; and quote validity. Record deviations instead of silently treating unlike proposals as equivalent.</p>
      <p>ASTM D6198 describes a broad transport-packaging design process that includes product, distribution hazards, packaging materials, testing and economics. For flexible barriers, ASTM methods such as D3985, F1249 and F88/F88M can define how specific properties are measured, but the buyer still needs product-specific requirements and acceptance criteria.</p>

      <h2>References and technical resources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://store.astm.org/d6198-18.html" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASTM D6198, Standard Guide for Transport Packaging Design</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://store.astm.org/d4169-22.html" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASTM D4169, Standard Practice for Performance Testing of Shipping Containers and Systems</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://store.astm.org/products-services/standards-and-publications/standards/paper-standards-and-packaging-standards.html" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASTM paper, shipping-container and flexible-barrier standards index</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://inspection.canada.ca/en/preventive-controls/food-establishments" rel="noopener noreferrer">CFIA guidance on food-packaging materials and supplier documentation</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <RelatedContent items={[
    { title: 'Request a Quote', href: '/contact', description: 'Send the completed RFQ with product files and delivery details.' },
    { title: 'Packaging Design Support', href: '/design-support', description: 'Prepare structural and artwork inputs before production.' },
    { title: 'Packaging Cost Drivers', href: '/blog/packaging-cost-drivers-box-quote-canada', description: 'Understand the variables that make supplier quotations differ.' }
  ]} />
  <CTABand
    heading="Send a packaging RFQ suppliers can answer clearly."
    lead="Complete the 12 fields, attach the product and artwork information, and identify which decisions are open to recommendation."
    primaryCta={{ label: 'Request a packaging quote', href: '/contact' }}
  />
</BaseLayout>
